!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/problem-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The error body this project writes when a request cannot be fulfilled -- an HTTP status, a short
  title, and the diagnostics that produced it.

  !!id convention (tson-java's CLAUDE.md, "Project-owned schema !!id convention"): /2026/32 is the
  spec revision this is written against, ltr8 the publishing org, http this project's schema group,
  and the trailing -1 this schema's own version. Per that convention's "bump it under a new name"
  rule, never edit a published schema's content in place -- a shape change becomes problem-2.tn.

  SUPERSEDED by problem-2.tn, which follows RFC 9457. Still published, and still valid: §10 makes a
  published schema immutable, so a document that named this one must go on resolving. Nothing new is
  written against it.

  diagnostic_code and diagnostic began as a copy of tson-cli's diagnostics.tn. They are no longer
  held to it: a CLI reports on files and a server reports on requests, and the two shapes are free to
  diverge where that difference matters.
  """
{
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION
    UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD SCHEMA_ERROR UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR
  ]
  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be absent -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1).
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path: text?
    schema_pointer: text?
    schema_id: text?
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected: text?
    actual: text?
    data_position: text?
    schema_position: text?
  }
  @doc:"""
    status is the HTTP status code the response carries, restated in the body so a stored or
    forwarded problem stays self-describing. title is a short, stable phrase for the class of
    failure; detail is free prose about this one occurrence, absent when the title says it all.

    errors is empty for a failure that produced no diagnostic at all -- an unsupported media type,
    an unacceptable Accept -- which is why it is a list rather than a required single value.
    """
  problem => {
    status: int32
    title: text
    detail: text?
    errors: [diagnostic]
  }
}
